.TH DEXD 1 "August 19, 2026" "dexd 0.1.0" "dexd manual"
.SH NAME
dexd \- play one HEVC video in an endless loop with no visible break
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dexd
.RI [ stream.265 ]
.RB [ \-\-fps
.IR F ]
.RB [ \-\-mode
.IR WxH@R ]
.RB [ \-\-exhibit\-config
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-defaults ]
.RB [ \-\-opt
.IR K=V " ...]"
.PP
.B dexd
.I stream.265
.B \-\-test\-rig\-no\-sidecar
.BI \-\-fps " F"
.RB [ \-\-mode
.IR WxH@R ]
.RB [ \-\-test\-rig\-force\-recovery\-after\-secs
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-test\-rig\-hang\-after\-secs
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-defaults ]
.RB [ \-\-opt
.IR K=V " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dexd
plays one raw HEVC video \(em a
.I .265
file \(em in an endless loop with no visible break at the loop point. dexd
feeds mpv the video as a stream that never ends: at the last byte it starts
again at the first, so mpv never reopens the file and the loop point costs no
frame.
.PP
A player runs for weeks unattended and is switched off at the mains. There is
no graceful shutdown: power is cut, and the player is built to survive that.
.PP
On a device the
.B dexd.service
unit starts the player with no arguments. Which video plays and how the
display is driven come from the exhibit config beside the video,
.I /opt/dex/exhibit.yaml
or
.IR /opt/dex/exhibit.json ,
and the frame rate comes from the sidecar beside the video.
.PP
A value given on the command line is a cross\-check: where it disagrees with
the config or the sidecar, dexd names both values and exits. Where nothing
names a video, a display mode or a frame rate, dexd refuses to start rather
than guess.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I stream.265
The video to play. Optional on a deployed player, where the exhibit config's
.B asset
line names it \(em which is what lets several videos sit in
.I /opt/dex
with the exhibit choosing one. Given here as well, it must resolve to the file
the config names.
.TP
.BI \-\-fps " F"
Cross\-check the frame rate, written as
.BR 30 ,
.B 29.97
or
.BR 30000/1001 .
A raw stream carries no timestamps, so the rate cannot be read from the video:
it comes from the sidecar, and dexd exits where the value here differs from
the sidecar's. The two are compared as written, so
.B 30
and
.B 30/1
disagree.
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " WxH@R"
Cross\-check the display mode against the exhibit config's
.B display_mode
key; where the two differ, dexd names both and exits. Alongside
.B \-\-test\-rig\-no\-sidecar
it is the only source, and
.B auto
\(em the mode the connector prefers \(em applies when it is omitted there.
.TP
.BI \-\-exhibit\-config " path"
Read the exhibit config from
.IR path .
Default: whichever of
.I /opt/dex/exhibit.yaml
and
.I /opt/dex/exhibit.json
exists. At most one of the two may exist: where both do, dexd names the file
to delete and exits rather than settle it by precedence. The extension selects
the parser \(em
.I .json
is strict JSON,
.I .yaml
and
.I .yml
are YAML \(em and the schema is the same either way.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-defaults
Omit the built\-in mpv option set described under NOTES. All of it goes, not
only the output path: mpv then decodes in software, which on a Raspberry Pi 4
is slower than realtime at 3840x2160, and it times the pictures itself, which
plays a
.I .265
file at the wrong speed with no error. The automated tests pass it together
with
.B \-\-opt vo=null
to run with no display; a deployed player never passes it.
.TP
.BI \-\-opt " K=V"
Pass mpv one more option. Repeatable, and applied after the built\-in set, so
an option named here replaces the built\-in value. Where mpv rejects an option,
dexd exits with status 2.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage text to standard error and exit with status 2.
.SS Test rig only
The three options below serve a test rig and are never passed on a deployed
player. dexd refuses the last two without
.BR \-\-test\-rig\-no\-sidecar ,
so neither can be armed against a video that is bound to its sidecar.
.TP
.B \-\-test\-rig\-no\-sidecar
Run without the sidecar and without the exhibit config, taking the frame rate
and the display mode from
.B \-\-fps
and
.BR \-\-mode .
.B \-\-fps
is required, the video must be named on the command line, and the check
against the kernel command line is skipped.
.TP
.BI \-\-test\-rig\-force\-recovery\-after\-secs " N"
Force an in\-place recovery \(em dexd reloading the video without exiting \(em
.I N
seconds after the request to load the video was queued, whether or not
anything has stalled. Decode startup takes time as well, so choose
.I N
with margin over it to exercise steady playback rather than startup.
.TP
.BI \-\-test\-rig\-hang\-after\-secs " N"
Hang the supervisor thread \(em dexd's one thread, which waits on mpv and runs
its own checks \(em forever,
.I N
seconds after startup. Neither the progress check nor the heartbeat reports a
hang there, so this option shows whether a systemd watchdog
.RB ( WatchdogSec= )
fires and restarts the process. dexd does not recover from it; systemd, or the
test harness, ends the process.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /opt/dex
The assets directory, and the mount point of the dex card's data partition. A
card in a computer shows the video, its sidecar and the exhibit config
together.
.TP
.IR /opt/dex/exhibit.yaml ", " /opt/dex/exhibit.json
The exhibit config. The package installs neither file; where none exists, dexd
prints the path to create with a minimal example and exits. Its keys:
.RS
.TP
.B asset
The video to play: a file name or a relative path, resolved against the
directory the config file is in, or an absolute path.
.TP
.B display_mode
Required. The display mode to ask for.
.TP
.B kms_force
The forced display mode the kernel command line must carry.
.TP
.B connector
The connector the display is plugged into.
.TP
.BR display ", " venue ", " note
Informational lines the player does not act on.
.RE
.TP
.I <stream.265>.json
The sidecar, required beside the video. It carries the frame rate, a checksum
of the video's bytes and, where the preparation tool could read them, the
video's width and height; dexd exits where the sidecar is missing, stale or
does not match the video. Written by
.BR dex\-sidecar (1).
.TP
.I /lib/systemd/system/dexd.service
The unit: what starts the player at boot, restarts it after a failure and arms
the watchdog.
.TP
.I /var/cache/dexd
mpv's cache directory, created by the unit and the one path the player writes.
.TP
.I /proc/cmdline
The running kernel command line. Its
.B video=
token must agree with the forced display mode and the connector in the exhibit
config;
.BR dex\-exhibit\-apply (1)
reconciles the two.
.TP
.I /sys/class/drm/card*\-<connector>/modes
The modes the connected display offers. dexd exits where
.B display_mode
names a mode absent from this list.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The player stopped without a failure. Normal operation does not reach this
status, because the stream never ends.
.TP
.B 1
Playback failed, mpv's event queue overflowed, or dexd reloaded the video in
place three times without the picture moving again. systemd restarts the
process.
.TP
.B 2
A startup check refused: a command line that was not understood, a missing or
invalid exhibit config, an exhibit config that disagrees with the kernel
command line, a
.B display_mode
the connector does not offer, a missing or mismatched sidecar, a video that
does not begin with a keyframe of the kind dexd needs (prepare it again), or
an option mpv rejected. Fix the cause and start the player again; a restart on
its own changes nothing.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Start with the last lines of the system log:
.PP
.EX
journalctl \-u dexd \-n 20
.EE
.PP
Every line below goes to standard error, which the unit sends to the system
log. A run that reaches playback prints the startup lines in the order below,
then a heartbeat line, and another every ten minutes after that.
.TP
.B dexd <version> (<commit>)
The first line of every run, printed before anything can fail, so a log read
weeks later names the build it came from.
.TP
.B "dexd: display <mode> (<source>), connector <name>, kms\-force <mode>"
The resolved display. The source is the exhibit config and its path, or a test
rig override.
.TP
.B "dexd: asset <path> (<source>)"
The video that will play, and whether the exhibit config or the command line
named it.
.TP
.B "dexd: <N> bytes, fps <F> (sidecar), looping endlessly"
The last check passed and playback begins.
.TP
.B "dexd: watchdog: armed (window <W>, ping cadence <N>s)"
Whether the systemd watchdog is on for this run. Outside a unit the line reads
.B "dexd: watchdog: inert (<reason>)"
and dexd sends no pings.
.TP
.B "dexd: heartbeat loops=143 uptime=3600s temp=48.2C frame\-drops=0 vo\-delayed=2 pos=3599.4s pos\-age=0s watchdog=armed pings\-dropped=0"
While this line keeps arriving the player is alive. Its fields:
.RS
.TP
.B loops
Loop iterations since startup.
.TP
.BR uptime ", " temp
How long the player has been running, and the chip temperature.
.TP
.BR frame\-drops ", " vo\-delayed
Frames the decoder dropped, and frames the display showed late.
.TP
.BR pos ", " pos\-age
The playback position, and how long ago it was sampled.
.TP
.BR watchdog ", " pings\-dropped
.B armed
with the count of pings that failed to send, or
.B inert
where the run has no systemd watchdog.
.RE
.IP
A counter reads
.B n/a
until its first value arrives and
.B off
where its subscription failed, so the first heartbeat reads
.B n/a
for everything mpv has not reported yet.
.TP
.B "dexd: health check: no progress across 2 consecutive checks (10s apart) \-\- attempting in\-place recovery 1/3"
Playback stopped advancing \(em dexd samples the position every ten seconds
\(em so dexd reloaded the video in place without exiting. The counter says how
many of the three attempts are spent. The following heartbeat lines show
whether the position moves again.
.TP
.B "dexd: fatal: playback ended (reason=<r>, error=<e>)"
Status 1. Playback of this stream can only end by failing, so dexd exits and
systemd restarts it.
.TP
.B "dexd: fatal: in\-place recovery exhausted its budget"
Status 1. dexd reloaded the video in place three times without the picture
moving again, so it exits and systemd restarts it. The three attempts never
refill.
.TP
.B "dexd: fatal: mpv event queue overflowed"
Status 1. mpv dropped events dexd needs to see, so dexd exits and systemd
restarts it.
.TP
.B "error: <what was refused>"
Status 2, printed before playback. The line names both values on a
disagreement, and the file to create or the command to run where one exists.
.TP
.B "warning: display_mode is ""auto"" and the asset is <WxH>"
The connector offers no mode of the video's own size, so it plays at whatever
size the connector negotiates while every other reading looks nominal. Set
.B display_mode
and
.B kms_force
in the exhibit config, run
.BR "sudo dex\-exhibit\-apply" ,
and reboot.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NOTIFY_SOCKET
Set by systemd for a unit that lets the service send it status messages. dexd
sends its watchdog pings there. Without it the watchdog is inert, which is the
normal state outside a unit.
.TP
.B WATCHDOG_USEC
Set by systemd from
.BR WatchdogSec= .
It is the window the pings must stay inside.
.TP
.B WATCHDOG_PID
Set by systemd. Where it is set and names another process \(em a wrapper
script rather than dexd \(em the watchdog is inert and dexd sends no pings.
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Where mpv keeps its cache. The unit sets it to
.IR /var/cache/dexd ,
the one directory the player may write.
.SH NOTES
The display mode belongs to the exhibit: the panel at the venue decides it,
not the video and not the unit, so it lives in the exhibit config beside the
video. After changing
.B display_mode
or
.B kms_force
there, run
.BR "sudo dex\-exhibit\-apply" \(em
.BR dex\-exhibit\-apply (1)
\(em and reboot, so the kernel command line and the exhibit config agree
again.
.PP
By default dexd sets the one mpv option set measured to play 3840x2160 at 30
fps in realtime on a Raspberry Pi 4: the decoded picture goes straight to the
display hardware through
.BR \-\-gpu\-hwdec\-interop=drmprime\-overlay ,
while every other path converts it first and falls short of realtime. The
measurement record in the repository,
.IR docs/design/measurements.md ,
holds the conditions.
